// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
; specs/analog/sar_dff_q.t27 -- the DC levels of the golden SAR deck's dff in its four stored states, on the PSP103 log tables, judged by ngspice
; M5g of the analog emulation epic (#10486, issue #11400). specs/analog/accept_sar.t27 applies the external team's per-block checks to our models; its dff block had one row it could not produce, the LEVELS of
; the eight observation points (Q and nQ after a reset, a capture of 1, a capture of 0 and a set), limit 50 mV from the rail. This file solves the cell's nodes on the same tt 27 C tables as the nand, the nor
; and the tgate (mos_psp103_gates.t27, M5d) and compares Q and nQ with ngspice-47's run of the same cell (specs/analog/decks/accept_dff.cir), against the same bar as the gates: 10 uV. Reused, not copied: the
; nor is `gt_nor`, the inverter is `dc_out` of mos_psp103.t27, the transmission gate's resistance and leakage are `gt_tg_ron` and `gt_tg_off`; what is new is the netlist, the sweep that closes its loop and
; the comparison.
; -
; THE CELL. The golden dff (pins VDD VSS Q CLK nQ D Reset Set): x10 inverts CLK; x1 passes D to net1 while CLK is LOW (the master is transparent while the clock is low, as in sar_logic.t27); x2 = nor(Set, net1)
; gives net2; x3 passes net2 to net3 while CLK is high; x4 = nor(Reset, net3) gives Q; x6 inverts Q to nQ; x5 = nor(Q, Set) gives net4; x7 passes net4 to net3 while CLK is low; x8 = nor(net2, Reset) gives net5;
; x9 passes net5 to net1 while CLK is high. The devices are those of the gates: NMOS 1.0 um, PMOS 3.0 um, L 0.45 um, tt, 27 C, the tables TT_LG_N and TT_LG_P. The deck reads each state with the clock low and
; Reset and Set low, so x1 and x7 are on and x3 and x9 are off, and D reaches net1. There are three circuits, not four: a stored 0 with D at 1.5 V (the reset: net1 = 1, net2 = 0, net3 = 1, Q = 0, nQ = 1, net4 = 1,
; net5 = 1), a stored 0 with D at 0 V (the capture of 0: net1 = 0, net2 = 1, net3 = 1, net4 = 1, net5 = 0) and a stored 1 with D at 0 V (the capture of 1 and the set, which are one circuit: net1 = 0, net2 = 1,
; net3 = 0, Q = 1, nQ = 0, net4 = 0, net5 = 0).
; THE SOLVE. In a stored state every node sits microvolts from a rail, so a gate sees its inputs at a rail and a node is the DC level of the gate that drives it: `gt_nor` for x2, x4, x5, x8 (its two inputs in the
; order the cell names them), `dc_out` for x6, and an on transmission gate passes its input (net1 is D, exactly). One load is in the tables and enters: the off x3 has net2 on one side and net3 on the other, on
; opposite rails whenever D opposes the stored bit (the reset and the stored 1), and leaks `gt_tg_off` (291.0 pA at full swing) from the high side to the low side; that current flows through x2's on devices on net2's
; side and through x7 (on, its `gt_tg_ron`) and x5's on devices on net3's side (`dq_rout`, a small-signal read at 10 mV of the on pull network: two PMOS in series for a 1, one NMOS for a 0), which moves net2, net3 and net4
; away from their rails. The off x9 has net5 and net1 on the same rail in every state and leaks nothing (to the word, a test). A sweep over the seven nodes closes the loop net3 -> Q -> net4 -> net3; it is at its fixed point at
; once, and a test pins that one and six sweeps give the same words. Voltages are words of 2^-24 V (59.6 nV). Q and nQ are the two numbers the deck prints; net1 to net5 are derived with no ngspice number beside them and
; are context, not a row.
; -
; THE REFERENCE. specs/analog/decks/accept_dff.cir: the golden deck's own dff subcircuit (fetched at run time, never vendored), no load on Q or nQ, Reset high to 10 ns, CLK high 20 to 30 ns and 50 to 60 ns, D at
; 1.5 V to 40 ns then 0, Set high 70 to 80 ns; read at 15 ns (reset), 45 ns (capture 1), 68 ns (capture 0) and 95 ns (set), each 15 ns or less after an edge. ngspice-47, mos_tt, 27 C, the reference host. The
; deck prints each level as its distance from the rail, to 6 digits: the DQ_NG_Q and DQ_NG_NQ constants below, none changed after printing.
; -
; THE RESULT, our model against that run, in uV (the distance from the rail; ours minus ngspice's below it):
;                    reset      capture 1   capture 0   set
;   Q   ours         0.0596     1.8477      0.0596      1.8477
;       ngspice      1.3901    14.5814      1.3901     18.1517
;       gap         -1.3305   -12.7337     -1.3305    -16.3040
;   nQ  ours         0.2980     0.1192      0.2980      0.1192
;       ngspice      0.7207     0.6355      0.7207      0.6378
;       gap         -0.4227    -0.5163     -0.4227     -0.5186
;   The report's check, every level within 50 mV of its rail: ours worst 1.848 uV, ngspice's worst 18.152 uV, 27000 and 2750 times inside. The cross-check bar, 10 uV (GT_B_LEVEL_V): SIX OF THE EIGHT
;   ARE INSIDE IT (the largest gap of those six is 1.33 uV) AND TWO ARE NOT: Q's 1 level after a capture of 1 (12.73 uV under ngspice's) and after a set (16.30 uV under). accept_sar.t27 therefore keeps the row
;   NOT MODELLED, with these numbers beside it.
; -
; WHY, and what is not shown. All eight gaps are NEGATIVE: ours is below ngspice's, for a 1 node and for a 0 node. That is the sign of a DC current at the node that our tables do not carry: a load that draws a 1
; node down from its rail and a source that lifts a 0 node off its. The tables are drain currents Id(Vgs, Vds); the gate current is not in them (mos_psp103_data.t27: the gate current that leaves through the drain
; at Vds = 0 and Vgs above 1.2 V, at most 2 units = 0.6 nA, is held at 0, the table being unsigned). Q is the gate node of four devices (x5 and x6, an NMOS and a PMOS each) and nQ and net4 are their drains, so the
; current is there: read through the table's own on-resistance at the node (`dq_rout`: 3582.5 ohm for the two PMOS in series that hold a 1, 1301.5 ohm for the NMOS that holds a 0), the gap is a current of 3.55 nA (capture 1) and
; 4.55 nA (set) at Q's 1 level, 1.02 nA at Q's 0 level, 0.24 nA and 0.40 nA at nQ's 1 and 0 levels. That a gate current of that size is the whole of it is NOT shown here: no number for it is in the repository, and
; the deck prints nothing that would separate it from another cause. The gates of mos_psp103_gates.t27 show the same sign against the same ngspice (every nand and nor level of ours is below its, by 0.14 to 2.36 uV, in cells
; with no gate to drive), which is consistent with a gate current in the cell's own devices and does not prove one; the dff adds the gates Q drives. The deck's own two reads of ONE stored state differ: capture 1 and set are the same circuit (stored 1, D 0 V, Reset and Set low, clock
; low) and ngspice gives 14.58 and 18.15 uV for Q, 3.57 uV apart, where reset and capture 0 (the stored 0) agree to 0.04 nV; a DC solve has one answer for the pair, ours, so the two reads cannot both be the
; DC level, and the cause of the spread is not measured here (two candidates, neither tested: the series stacks' middle nodes float, with time constants of tens of ns, and the deck reads 4.8 to 14.8 ns after an edge).
; -
; THE NEGATIVE CONTROLS (FR-004), each a test that passes by seeing the failure, and ONE THAT DOES NOT: the limit of the check. The pull-up of x4 (its two PMOS) dead: Q's 1 level at 0 V, 1.5 V away; the NMOS table
; dead: Q's 0 level at 1.5 V, and Q's 1 level 1.4 mV off its rail; the pull-up of x4 at a HUNDREDTH of its width (`dq_scale`, 435412 words off the log table): Q's 1 level 186.4 uV, 171.8 uV from ngspice's, 17 times
; the bar: refused. THE LIMIT: the pull-up at a TENTH of its width (217706 words, log2 10 octaves): Q's 1 level 18.656 uV, 10.1 times ours and 4.07 uV and 0.50 uV from ngspice's capture 1 and set, so all
; eight levels are inside the bar (the worst gap 4.07 uV): the check ACCEPTS the cell with a pull-up ten times too weak, and refuses the cell as the tables give it. The width control the issue asks for (a tenth
; of the width refused) is not met on these tables, and the reason is the one above: the level that ngspice reads at Q's 1 includes a load current worth 13 to 16 uV, and a pull-up ten times weaker lands on it by
; accident. So the bar of 10 uV is NOT loosened to make the row a PASS, and no PASS is claimed: a PASS here would be as likely a wrong cell as a right one.
; -
; WHAT THE RESULT SAYS. On the tt 27 C PSP103 log tables, with the gate current absent, the dff's Q and nQ sit within 2 uV of a rail in every stored state, inside the report's 50 mV by 27000 times, and six of
; the eight are within 1.33 uV of ngspice's own run of the same cell. WHAT IT DOES NOT SAY. That two of the eight are within the bar (they are 12.7 and 16.3 uV under it); that the gate current is the cause; the
; levels of net1 to net5 (no ngspice number); another corner, temperature or load on Q or nQ; the dff with its clock high, or a level in a transition; speed (the clock-to-Q is the first stage of the sequencer in
; accept_sar.t27). The model is a TABLE MODEL of PSP103 without the gate current, not PSP103. ASCII only, English only.
; phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

module AnalogSarDffQ;

use analog::linear::{fabs64};
use analog::mos_table::{VDD, lg_fine, dpos, fmax64, LG_R_OFF, LG_LEN};
use analog::mos_psp103::{UPV, IUNIT_A, dc_out};
use analog::mos_psp103_data::{TT_LG_N, TT_LG_P};
use analog::mos_psp103_gates::{GT_DV, GT_B_LEVEL_V, gt_nor, gt_tg_ron, gt_tg_off};

pub const KIND : str = "analog-cell-check";
pub const ID : str = "analog/sar_dff_q";
pub const REFERENCE : str = "IHP-Open-PDK 21133f21 sg13_lv_nmos/pmos (PSP103) through ngspice-47; deck specs/analog/decks/accept_dff.cir";

/// The four stored states the deck measures, in the order of its prints: after a reset (Reset high then low, D 1.5 V), after a capture of 1 (D = 1.5 V sampled, D then 0), after a capture of 0
/// (D = 0 sampled) and after a set (Set high then low, D 0). The clock is low and Reset and Set are low in all four.
pub const DQ_RESET : u32 = 0;
pub const DQ_CAP1 : u32 = 1;
pub const DQ_CAP0 : u32 = 2;
pub const DQ_SET : u32 = 3;

; --- ngspice-47 + PSP103 mos_tt 27 C, specs/analog/decks/accept_dff.cir (6 digits as printed, none changed) --------------------------------
/// Q's distance from its rail in V in the four states (for a 1, 1.5 V minus Q; for a 0, Q itself): reset, capture 1, capture 0, set.
pub const DQ_NG_Q : [4]f64 = [1.39007e-06, 1.45814e-05, 1.39011e-06, 1.81517e-05];
/// nQ's, in the same order.
pub const DQ_NG_NQ : [4]f64 = [7.2068e-07, 6.35462e-07, 7.20689e-07, 6.37802e-07];

; --- the stored states -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// A stored 1 in state s: after a capture of 1 and after a set.
pub fn dq_one(s: u32) -> bool {
    return s == DQ_CAP1 or s == DQ_SET;
}

/// D is high (1.5 V) when the state is read: only in the reset state (D falls at 40 ns and the other three are read after it).
pub fn dq_d_high(s: u32) -> bool {
    return s == DQ_RESET;
}

/// The rail of a node: VDD for a 1, 0 V for a 0, in words of 2^-24 V.
pub fn dq_rail(high: bool) -> u32 {
    if (high) { return VDD; }
    return 0;
}

/// A node's distance from the rail it should sit on, in V: for a 1 the supply minus the node, for a 0 the node (the digits fall on the deviation and not on the 1.5 V).
pub fn dq_dev(w: u32, high: bool) -> f64 {
    if (high) { return ((VDD as f64) - (w as f64)) / UPV; }
    return (w as f64) / UPV;
}

/// A node moved dw words away from its rail (a 1 down, a 0 up), at most to the other rail.
pub fn dq_away(w: u32, high: bool, dw: u32) -> u32 {
    if (high) { return dpos(w as u64, dw as u64) as u32; }
    if (w + dw > VDD) { return VDD; }
    return w + dw;
}

/// A voltage in words from a current and a resistance, rounded; a dead device (no current, an infinite or undefined resistance) is the supply.
pub fn dq_words(i_a: f64, r_ohm: f64) -> u32 {
    const w : f64 = i_a * r_ohm * UPV + 0.5;
    if (w < (VDD as f64)) { return w as u32; }
    return VDD;
}

/// The output resistance of a nor whose output sits on a rail through its on devices, at a small signal (10 mV): a 1 through the two PMOS in series (both inputs low), a 0 through one NMOS.
pub fn dq_rout(tn: []const u32, tp: []const u32, one: bool) -> f64 {
    const dv : f64 = (GT_DV as f64) / UPV;
    if (one) { return 2.0 * dv / ((lg_fine(tp, VDD, GT_DV) as f64) * IUNIT_A); }
    return dv / ((lg_fine(tn, VDD, GT_DV) as f64) * IUNIT_A);
}

/// The table t with every current divided by 2^(cut / 65536) (the log words lowered by cut, floored at 0 = 1 IUNIT; the tag and the rv words unchanged): a device of that fraction of the
/// width, in the weak and the strong region alike. out has the length of a log table, 4851 words. cut = 217706 is a tenth (log2 10 = 3.32193 octaves of 65536 words), 435412 a hundredth.
pub fn dq_scale(t: []const u32, out: []u32, cut: u32) {
    out[0] = t[0];
    var i : usize = 1;
    while (i < LG_R_OFF) {
        out[i] = dpos((t[i]) as u64, (cut as u64)) as u32;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    while (i < LG_LEN) {
        out[i] = t[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

; --- the solve ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// The nodes of the dff in a stored state, in words of 2^-24 V.
pub struct DqNodes { net1: u32, net2: u32, net3: u32, net4: u32, net5: u32, q: u32, nq: u32 }

/// The nodes of the stored 1 (one) or the stored 0, with D at the rail d_high, the clock low and Reset and Set low, by `iters` sweeps from the ideal rails. tn and tp are the tables of every device;
/// tp4 is the PMOS table of x4's pull-up alone (the same as tp for the cell, another for a control).
pub fn dq_solve_k(tn: []const u32, tp: []const u32, tp4: []const u32, one: bool, d_high: bool, iters: u32) -> DqNodes {
    // the rails: with the clock low x1 and x7 are on (D reaches net1, net4 reaches net3) and x3 and x9 are off; Reset and Set are low, so net2 = nor(0, D) is high when D is low, net5 = nor(net2, 0) is
    // its complement, net4 = nor(Q, 0) and net3 are the complement of Q. x9 has net5 on one side and net1 = D on the other: the same rail in every state, so it leaks nothing
    const hi2 : bool = !d_high;
    const hi3 : bool = !one;
    var n1 : u32 = dq_rail(d_high);
    var n2 : u32 = dq_rail(hi2);
    var n3 : u32 = dq_rail(hi3);
    var n4 : u32 = dq_rail(hi3);
    var n5 : u32 = dq_rail(d_high);
    var q : u32 = dq_rail(one);
    var nq : u32 = dq_rail(hi3);
    // the off x3 has net2 on one side and net3 on the other: on opposite rails (D against the stored bit) it leaks gt_tg_off, 1.5 V across it, and the current flows through x2's on devices on net2's side
    // and through x7 (on) and x5's on devices on net3's side, which moves both nodes away from their rails
    var i_leak : f64 = 0.0;
    if (one != d_high) { i_leak = gt_tg_off(tn, tp); }
    var v7 : u32 = 0;
    if (hi3) { v7 = VDD - GT_DV; }
    const dw2 : u32 = dq_words(i_leak, dq_rout(tn, tp, hi2));
    const dw4 : u32 = dq_words(i_leak, dq_rout(tn, tp, hi3));
    const dw7 : u32 = dq_words(i_leak, gt_tg_ron(tn, tp, v7, true));
    var it : u32 = 0;
    while (it < iters) {
        n2 = dq_away(gt_nor(tn, tp, 0, n1), hi2, dw2);
        n5 = gt_nor(tn, tp, n2, 0);
        q = gt_nor(tn, tp4, 0, n3);
        nq = dc_out(tn, tp, q);
        n4 = dq_away(gt_nor(tn, tp, q, 0), hi3, dw4);
        n3 = dq_away(n4, hi3, dw7);
        it = it + 1;
    }
    return DqNodes { net1: n1, net2: n2, net3: n3, net4: n4, net5: n5, q: q, nq: nq };
}

/// The solve with the three sweeps it needs: a node moves only by the words of a rail-to-rail gate level or of a leakage times a resistance, so the sweep reaches its fixed point at once and a test
/// pins that more sweeps change nothing.
pub fn dq_solve(tn: []const u32, tp: []const u32, tp4: []const u32, one: bool, d_high: bool) -> DqNodes {
    return dq_solve_k(tn, tp, tp4, one, d_high, 3);
}

; --- the comparison ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// Our Q and nQ in a state, as the distance from their rails in V, and the signed gap to ngspice's (ours minus its).
pub struct DqPair { q_v: f64, nq_v: f64, gq_v: f64, gnq_v: f64 }

pub fn dq_pair(tn: []const u32, tp: []const u32, tp4: []const u32, s: u32) -> DqPair {
    const one : bool = dq_one(s);
    const d : DqNodes = dq_solve(tn, tp, tp4, one, dq_d_high(s));
    const q : f64 = dq_dev(d.q, one);
    const nq : f64 = dq_dev(d.nq, !one);
    return DqPair { q_v: q, nq_v: nq, gq_v: q - DQ_NG_Q[s as usize], gnq_v: nq - DQ_NG_NQ[s as usize] };
}

/// The eight levels: worst_v, the largest distance of any of our eight from its rail (the report's check, 50 mV), gap_v, the largest distance of any of ours from ngspice's (the cross-check, 10 uV),
/// and n_in, how many of the eight are within that bar of ngspice's.
pub struct DqLv { worst_v: f64, gap_v: f64, n_in: u32 }

pub fn dq_levels(tn: []const u32, tp: []const u32, tp4: []const u32) -> DqLv {
    var worst : f64 = 0.0;
    var gap : f64 = 0.0;
    var n_in : u32 = 0;
    var s : u32 = 0;
    while (s < 4) {
        const p : DqPair = dq_pair(tn, tp, tp4, s);
        worst = fmax64(worst, fmax64(p.q_v, p.nq_v));
        gap = fmax64(gap, fmax64(fabs64(p.gq_v), fabs64(p.gnq_v)));
        if (fabs64(p.gq_v) <= GT_B_LEVEL_V) { n_in = n_in + 1; }
        if (fabs64(p.gnq_v) <= GT_B_LEVEL_V) { n_in = n_in + 1; }
        s = s + 1;
    }
    return DqLv { worst_v: worst, gap_v: gap, n_in: n_in };
}

; --- the tests ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Variables, not constants, carry the data of the asserts: an assert on a constant is folded by the compiler and counts as no assert at all.

test the_dff_nodes_sit_microvolts_from_their_rails_in_the_three_stored_circuits_and_a_first_and_a_sixth_sweep_change_no_word {
    // the stored 0 after a reset (D 1.5 V), the distances from the rail in words (59.6 nV): net1 is D, exactly; net2 7 (the gate level, 1 word, and 6 words = 0.36 uV: the leakage of the off x3, 291 pA, through x2's
    // NMOS, 1301 ohm); net3 57 and net4 48 (the nor 00 level 31 words, plus 17 words = 1.04 uV through x5's two PMOS in series, 3583 ohm, and 9 more through x7, 1791 ohm); net5 31 (the nor 00 level); Q 1; nQ 5
    const r : DqNodes = dq_solve(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, false, true);
    assert_eq(r.net1, VDD);
    assert_eq(r.net2, 7);
    assert_eq(VDD - r.net3, 57);
    assert_eq(VDD - r.net4, 48);
    assert_eq(VDD - r.net5, 31);
    assert_eq(r.q, 1);
    assert_eq(VDD - r.nq, 5);
    // the stored 0 after a capture of 0 (D 0 V): net2 and x3's other side are on the same rail, no leakage, so every node is its gate's level: net2, net3 and net4 31 words, net5 and Q 1 word, nQ 5
    const c0 : DqNodes = dq_solve(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, false, false);
    assert_eq(c0.net1, 0);
    assert_eq(VDD - c0.net2, 31);
    assert_eq(VDD - c0.net3, 31);
    assert_eq(VDD - c0.net4, 31);
    assert_eq(c0.net5, 1);
    assert_eq(c0.q, 1);
    assert_eq(VDD - c0.nq, 5);
    // the stored 1 (D 0 V): net2 48 (31 words, and 17 words under it through x2's two PMOS in series), net3 13 and net4 7 (1 word, and 6 words = 0.36 uV through x5's NMOS and 6 more through x7's NMOS), net5 1,
    // Q 31 (the nor 00 level), nQ 2
    const o : DqNodes = dq_solve(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, true, false);
    assert_eq(o.net1, 0);
    assert_eq(VDD - o.net2, 48);
    assert_eq(o.net3, 13);
    assert_eq(o.net4, 7);
    assert_eq(o.net5, 1);
    assert_eq(VDD - o.q, 31);
    assert_eq(o.nq, 2);
    // the sweep is at its fixed point: one and six sweeps give the same seven words, in the two circuits that carry the leakage of x3
    const r6 : DqNodes = dq_solve_k(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, false, true, 6);
    const o6 : DqNodes = dq_solve_k(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, true, false, 6);
    const r1 : DqNodes = dq_solve_k(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, false, true, 1);
    const o1 : DqNodes = dq_solve_k(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, true, false, 1);
    assert_eq(r6.net2, r.net2);
    assert_eq(r6.net3, r.net3);
    assert_eq(r6.net4, r.net4);
    assert_eq(r6.net5, r.net5);
    assert_eq(r6.q, r.q);
    assert_eq(r6.nq, r.nq);
    assert_eq(o6.net2, o.net2);
    assert_eq(o6.net3, o.net3);
    assert_eq(o6.net4, o.net4);
    assert_eq(o6.net5, o.net5);
    assert_eq(o6.q, o.q);
    assert_eq(o6.nq, o.nq);
    assert_eq(r1.net3, r.net3);
    assert_eq(r1.q, r.q);
    assert_eq(o1.net3, o.net3);
    assert_eq(o1.net4, o.net4);
    assert_eq(o1.q, o.q);
    assert_eq(o1.nq, o.nq);
}

test the_three_circuits_have_one_answer_each_and_the_off_x9_carries_nothing_and_x4s_input_net3_is_off_its_rail_and_nothing_moves {
    // reset and capture 0 are one stored 0 (they differ by D, which reaches net1, net2 and net5 and not Q or nQ): the same Q and nQ words; capture 1 and set are one circuit: the same seven words
    const s0 : DqNodes = dq_solve(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, dq_one(DQ_RESET), dq_d_high(DQ_RESET));
    const s2 : DqNodes = dq_solve(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, dq_one(DQ_CAP0), dq_d_high(DQ_CAP0));
    const s1 : DqNodes = dq_solve(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, dq_one(DQ_CAP1), dq_d_high(DQ_CAP1));
    const s3 : DqNodes = dq_solve(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, dq_one(DQ_SET), dq_d_high(DQ_SET));
    assert_eq(s0.q, s2.q);
    assert_eq(s0.nq, s2.nq);
    assert_eq(s1.q, s3.q);
    assert_eq(s1.nq, s3.nq);
    assert_eq(s1.net1, s3.net1);
    assert_eq(s1.net2, s3.net2);
    assert_eq(s1.net3, s3.net3);
    assert_eq(s1.net4, s3.net4);
    assert_eq(s1.net5, s3.net5);
    assert(dq_one(DQ_CAP1));
    assert(dq_one(DQ_SET));
    assert(!dq_one(DQ_RESET));
    assert(!dq_one(DQ_CAP0));
    assert(dq_d_high(DQ_RESET));
    assert(!dq_d_high(DQ_CAP1));
    // x9 off: net5 and net1 sit on the same rail in every state, 31 words apart at the most (the reset: net5 is 31 words under the supply, net1 is the supply), and at that voltage the off tgate leaks less
    // than half an IUNIT (1.164 pA): no current to a word. x1 on: net1 is D, exactly
    var dv0 : u32 = VDD - s0.net5;
    var dv1 : u32 = s1.net5;
    assert_eq(dv0, 31);
    assert_eq(dv1, 1);
    assert_eq(lg_fine(&TT_LG_N, 0, dv0) + lg_fine(&TT_LG_P, 0, dv0), 0);
    assert_eq(lg_fine(&TT_LG_N, 0, dv1) + lg_fine(&TT_LG_P, 0, dv1), 0);
    assert_eq(s0.net1, VDD);
    assert_eq(s1.net1, 0);
    assert_eq(s2.net1, 0);
    // x4's input net3 sits 57 words (3.4 uV) off its rail in the stored 0 after a reset and 13 words off it in the stored 1, and nothing moves: Q is the gate level with the input exactly on the rail, in both
    // circuits, and nQ is the inverter on Q
    var lo : u32 = 0;
    var hi : u32 = VDD;
    assert_eq(s0.q, gt_nor(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, lo, hi));
    assert_eq(s1.q, gt_nor(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, lo, lo));
    assert_eq(s0.nq, dc_out(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, s0.q));
    assert_eq(s1.nq, dc_out(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, s1.q));
    // the order of x4's two inputs is not resolved by the repository and does not matter: nor(0, 1) and nor(1, 0) are the same word (1 word over ground), the inputs at the rails
    assert_eq(gt_nor(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, lo, hi), gt_nor(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, hi, lo));
    assert_eq(gt_nor(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, lo, hi), 1);
}

test the_eight_levels_against_ngspice_six_are_within_1_4_uV_and_q_at_1_after_a_capture_of_1_and_after_a_set_is_12_7_and_16_3_uV_under {
    // reset: Q 0.0596 uV against 1.3901, nQ (a 1) 0.2980 uV against 0.7207
    const p0 : DqPair = dq_pair(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, DQ_RESET);
    assert(p0.q_v > 0.0000000596);
    assert(p0.q_v < 0.0000000597);
    assert(p0.nq_v > 0.000000298);
    assert(p0.nq_v < 0.0000002981);
    assert(p0.gq_v > 0.0 - 0.0000013305);
    assert(p0.gq_v < 0.0 - 0.0000013304);
    assert(p0.gnq_v > 0.0 - 0.00000042266);
    assert(p0.gnq_v < 0.0 - 0.00000042265);
    // capture 1: Q (a 1) 1.8477 uV against 14.5814, nQ (a 0) 0.1192 uV against 0.6355
    const p1 : DqPair = dq_pair(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, DQ_CAP1);
    assert(p1.q_v > 0.0000018477);
    assert(p1.q_v < 0.0000018478);
    assert(p1.nq_v > 0.00000011920);
    assert(p1.nq_v < 0.00000011921);
    assert(p1.gq_v > 0.0 - 0.0000127337);
    assert(p1.gq_v < 0.0 - 0.0000127336);
    assert(p1.gnq_v > 0.0 - 0.00000051626);
    assert(p1.gnq_v < 0.0 - 0.00000051625);
    // capture 0: Q 0.0596 uV against 1.3901, nQ 0.2980 uV against 0.7207
    const p2 : DqPair = dq_pair(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, DQ_CAP0);
    assert(p2.q_v > 0.0000000596);
    assert(p2.q_v < 0.0000000597);
    assert(p2.nq_v > 0.000000298);
    assert(p2.nq_v < 0.0000002981);
    assert(p2.gq_v > 0.0 - 0.0000013306);
    assert(p2.gq_v < 0.0 - 0.0000013305);
    assert(p2.gnq_v > 0.0 - 0.00000042267);
    assert(p2.gnq_v < 0.0 - 0.00000042266);
    // set: Q 1.8477 uV against 18.1517, nQ 0.1192 uV against 0.6378
    const p3 : DqPair = dq_pair(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, DQ_SET);
    assert(p3.q_v > 0.0000018477);
    assert(p3.q_v < 0.0000018478);
    assert(p3.nq_v > 0.00000011920);
    assert(p3.nq_v < 0.00000011921);
    assert(p3.gq_v > 0.0 - 0.0000163040);
    assert(p3.gq_v < 0.0 - 0.0000163039);
    assert(p3.gnq_v > 0.0 - 0.00000051860);
    assert(p3.gnq_v < 0.0 - 0.00000051859);
    // the six inside the bar are inside it by 7 times or more (the largest gap of the six, 1.3306 uV); the two Q levels at 1 are 1.27 and 1.63 times the bar outside it
    assert(fabs64(p0.gq_v) * 7.0 < GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    assert(fabs64(p2.gq_v) * 7.0 < GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    assert(fabs64(p0.gnq_v) * 20.0 < GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    assert(fabs64(p1.gnq_v) * 19.0 < GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    assert(fabs64(p2.gnq_v) * 20.0 < GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    assert(fabs64(p3.gnq_v) * 19.0 < GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    assert(fabs64(p1.gq_v) > 1.27 * GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    assert(fabs64(p1.gq_v) < 1.28 * GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    assert(fabs64(p3.gq_v) > 1.63 * GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    assert(fabs64(p3.gq_v) < 1.64 * GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    // the cross-check: the worst distance from a rail of ours is 1.8477 uV, the largest gap 16.304 uV, six of the eight inside the bar
    const lv : DqLv = dq_levels(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P);
    assert(lv.worst_v > 0.0000018477);
    assert(lv.worst_v < 0.0000018478);
    assert(lv.gap_v > 0.0000163039);
    assert(lv.gap_v < 0.0000163040);
    assert_eq(lv.n_in, 6);
    assert(lv.gap_v > GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    // ngspice's own worst level is 18.1517 uV: the report's 50 mV is 2750 times above it and 27000 times above ours
    var ng_worst : f64 = 0.0;
    var k : u32 = 0;
    while (k < 4) {
        ng_worst = fmax64(ng_worst, fmax64(DQ_NG_Q[k as usize], DQ_NG_NQ[k as usize]));
        k = k + 1;
    }
    assert(ng_worst > 0.00001815);
    assert(ng_worst < 0.00001816);
    var limit : f64 = 0.05;
    assert(ng_worst * 2700.0 < limit);
    assert(lv.worst_v * 27000.0 < limit);
}

test every_one_of_the_eight_gaps_is_negative_the_sign_of_a_dc_current_the_tables_do_not_carry_and_the_current_it_would_take_is_a_few_nA_at_q_when_it_is_a_1 {
    var k : u32 = 0;
    var neg : u32 = 0;
    while (k < 4) {
        const p : DqPair = dq_pair(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, k);
        if (p.gq_v < 0.0) { neg = neg + 1; }
        if (p.gnq_v < 0.0) { neg = neg + 1; }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    assert_eq(neg, 8);
    // the table's own resistance at the node: two PMOS in series at a 1, one NMOS at a 0 (10 mV across, full gate drive)
    const rp : f64 = dq_rout(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, true);
    const rn : f64 = dq_rout(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, false);
    assert(rp > 3582.5);
    assert(rp < 3582.6);
    assert(rn > 1301.4);
    assert(rn < 1301.5);
    // the current that would close the gap: gap over resistance, 3.554 nA (capture 1) and 4.551 nA (set) at Q's 1, 1.022 nA at Q's 0 (reset and capture 0), 0.236 nA at nQ's 1 (one PMOS: half of rp)
    // and 0.397 and 0.399 nA at nQ's 0
    const p0 : DqPair = dq_pair(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, DQ_RESET);
    const p1 : DqPair = dq_pair(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, DQ_CAP1);
    const p3 : DqPair = dq_pair(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, DQ_SET);
    const i_c1 : f64 = (0.0 - p1.gq_v) / rp;
    const i_set : f64 = (0.0 - p3.gq_v) / rp;
    const i_q0 : f64 = (0.0 - p0.gq_v) / rn;
    const i_nq1 : f64 = (0.0 - p0.gnq_v) / (rp / 2.0);
    const i_nq0 : f64 = (0.0 - p1.gnq_v) / rn;
    assert(i_c1 > 0.000000003554);
    assert(i_c1 < 0.000000003555);
    assert(i_set > 0.000000004550);
    assert(i_set < 0.000000004552);
    assert(i_q0 > 0.000000001022);
    assert(i_q0 < 0.000000001023);
    assert(i_nq1 > 0.000000000235);
    assert(i_nq1 < 0.000000000237);
    assert(i_nq0 > 0.000000000396);
    assert(i_nq0 < 0.000000000398);
    // the leakage of the off x1 that IS in the tables, for scale: 291.04 pA, 12 to 16 times smaller than what Q's 1 level would need
    const leak : f64 = gt_tg_off(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P);
    assert(leak > 0.000000000291);
    assert(leak < 0.0000000002911);
    assert(i_c1 > 12.0 * leak);
    assert(i_set > 15.0 * leak);
}

test ngspices_own_two_reads_of_one_stored_state_differ_by_3_57_uV_and_the_two_reads_of_the_other_agree_to_a_hundredth_of_a_nanovolt {
    // capture 1 and set are the same circuit (D 0 V, Reset and Set low, the clock low): Q 14.5814 and 18.1517 uV, 3.5703 uV apart; nQ 0.635462 and 0.637802 uV, 2.34 nV apart
    var i_set : usize = DQ_SET as usize;
    var i_c1 : usize = DQ_CAP1 as usize;
    var i_c0 : usize = DQ_CAP0 as usize;
    var i_rs : usize = DQ_RESET as usize;
    const dq : f64 = DQ_NG_Q[i_set] - DQ_NG_Q[i_c1];
    const dn : f64 = DQ_NG_NQ[i_set] - DQ_NG_NQ[i_c1];
    assert(dq > 0.0000035702);
    assert(dq < 0.0000035704);
    assert(dn > 0.0000000023);
    assert(dn < 0.0000000024);
    // reset and capture 0 are the other circuit: Q 1.39007 and 1.39011 uV, 0.04 nV apart; nQ 0.72068 and 0.720689 uV, 9 pV apart
    const rq : f64 = DQ_NG_Q[i_c0] - DQ_NG_Q[i_rs];
    const rn : f64 = DQ_NG_NQ[i_c0] - DQ_NG_NQ[i_rs];
    assert(rq > 0.00000000003);
    assert(rq < 0.00000000005);
    assert(rn > 0.000000000008);
    assert(rn < 0.00000000001);
    // a DC solve has one answer for the pair, so no model can be at the same time within 0 of both reads; the bar (10 uV) is 2.8 times the spread
    assert(GT_B_LEVEL_V > 2.8 * dq);
    assert(GT_B_LEVEL_V < 2.9 * dq);
}

test a_dead_pull_up_or_a_dead_nmos_or_a_pull_up_at_a_hundredth_of_the_width_is_refused_and_a_pull_up_at_a_tenth_of_the_width_is_not_that_is_the_limit_of_the_level_check {
    var dead : [4851]u32 = [0] ** 4851;
    var tenth : [4851]u32 = [0] ** 4851;
    var hundredth : [4851]u32 = [0] ** 4851;
    dq_scale(&TT_LG_P, &tenth, 217706);
    dq_scale(&TT_LG_P, &hundredth, 435412);
    // the scaled tables are a tenth and a hundredth of the current at full drive (258951843 IUNIT at 1.5 V, 1.5 V), to a part in a million, with the tag and the rv words untouched
    const full : u64 = lg_fine(&TT_LG_P, VDD, VDD);
    const t10 : u64 = lg_fine(&tenth, VDD, VDD);
    const t100 : u64 = lg_fine(&hundredth, VDD, VDD);
    assert(full > 258951000);
    assert(full < 258952000);
    assert(t10 * 10 > full - 1000);
    assert(t10 * 10 < full + 1000);
    assert(t100 * 100 > full - 10000);
    assert(t100 * 100 < full + 10000);
    assert_eq(tenth[0], TT_LG_P[0]);
    assert_eq(tenth[4800], TT_LG_P[4800]);
    // NEGATIVE CONTROL, the pull-up of x4 dead (a zero table in its slot): Q at a 1 never rises, it is 1.5 V from its rail, and so is nQ; the gap to ngspice's is 1.5 V, 150000 times the bar
    const d4 : DqLv = dq_levels(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &dead);
    assert(d4.worst_v > 1.4999);
    assert(d4.gap_v > 1.4999);
    assert(d4.gap_v > 100000.0 * GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    assert_eq(d4.n_in, 4);
    // NEGATIVE CONTROL, no NMOS conducts: Q at a 0 stays at 1.5 V (the pull-up wins), Q at a 1 is 1.4 mV off its rail, nQ at a 0 stays at the supply (1.5 V off)
    const dn : DqLv = dq_levels(&dead, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P);
    assert(dn.worst_v > 1.4999);
    assert(dn.gap_v > 1.4999);
    assert_eq(dn.n_in, 2);
    // NEGATIVE CONTROL, the pull-up of x4 at a HUNDREDTH of its width: Q at a 1 is 186.38 uV from its rail, 171.8 and 168.2 uV from ngspice's two reads, 17 times the bar: refused
    const h : DqLv = dq_levels(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &hundredth);
    const h1 : DqPair = dq_pair(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &hundredth, DQ_CAP1);
    assert(h1.q_v > 0.000186383);
    assert(h1.q_v < 0.000186384);
    assert(h.gap_v > 0.0001718);
    assert(h.gap_v < 0.0001719);
    assert(h.gap_v > 17.0 * GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    assert_eq(h.n_in, 6);
    // THE LIMIT OF THE CHECK: the pull-up at a TENTH of its width. Q at a 1 is 18.656 uV, 10.097 times ours (the model sees the width: Q at a 1 is the NMOS leakage over the pull-up's resistance), and it
    // is +4.07 uV from ngspice's capture 1 and +0.50 uV from its set: all eight levels are inside the 10 uV bar, the worst gap 4.07 uV. The check refuses the cell as the tables give it (gap 16.30 uV,
    // above) and ACCEPTS the one with a pull-up ten times too weak
    const t : DqLv = dq_levels(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &tenth);
    const t1 : DqPair = dq_pair(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &tenth, DQ_CAP1);
    const t3 : DqPair = dq_pair(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &tenth, DQ_SET);
    const b1 : DqPair = dq_pair(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P, DQ_CAP1);
    assert(t1.q_v > 0.000018656);
    assert(t1.q_v < 0.000018657);
    assert(t1.q_v > 10.09 * b1.q_v);
    assert(t1.q_v < 10.10 * b1.q_v);
    assert(t1.gq_v > 0.0000040748);
    assert(t1.gq_v < 0.0000040749);
    assert(t3.gq_v > 0.00000050455);
    assert(t3.gq_v < 0.00000050456);
    assert(t.gap_v > 0.0000040748);
    assert(t.gap_v < 0.0000040749);
    assert(t.gap_v < GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    assert_eq(t.n_in, 8);
    const base : DqLv = dq_levels(&TT_LG_N, &TT_LG_P, &TT_LG_P);
    assert(base.gap_v > GT_B_LEVEL_V);
    assert(base.gap_v > t.gap_v);
}
